.Sh DESCRIPTION
This command processes DSA keys.
They can be converted between various forms and their components printed
out.
.Sy Note
This command uses the traditional SSLeay compatible format for private
key encryption: newer applications should use the more secure PKCS#8
format using the
.Sy pkcs8
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -help
Print out a usage message.
.It Sy -inform Sy DER Ns | Ns Sy PEM
The key input format; unspecified by default.
See
.Xr openssl-format-options 1
for details.
.It Sy -outform Sy DER Ns | Ns Sy PEM
The key output format; the default is
.Sy PEM .
See
.Xr openssl-format-options 1
for details.
.Pp
Private keys are a sequence of
.Sy ASN.1 INTEGERS :
the version (zero),
.Sy p ,
.Sy q ,
.Sy g ,
and the public and private key components.
Public keys are a
.Sy SubjectPublicKeyInfo
structure with the
.Sy DSA
type.
.Pp
The
.Sy PEM
format also accepts PKCS#8 data.
.It Sy -in Em filename
This specifies the input filename to read a key from or standard input
if this option is not specified.
If the key is encrypted a pass phrase will be prompted for.
.It Sy -out Em filename
This specifies the output filename to write a key to or standard output
by is not specified.
If any encryption options are set then a pass phrase will be prompted
for.
The output filename should
.Sy not
be the same as the input filename.
.It Sy -passin Em arg Ns , Sy -passout Em arg
The password source for the input and output file.
For more information about the format of
.Sy arg
see
.Xr openssl-passphrase-options 1 .
.It Sy -aes128 Ns , Sy -aes192 Ns , Sy -aes256 Ns , Sy -aria128 Ns , Sy -aria192 Ns , Sy -aria256 Ns , Sy -camellia128 Ns , Sy -camellia192 Ns , Sy -camellia256 Ns , Sy -des Ns , Sy -des3 Ns , Sy -idea
These options encrypt the private key with the specified cipher before
outputting it.
A pass phrase is prompted for.
If none of these options is specified the key is written in plain text.
This means that this command can be used to remove the pass phrase from
a key by not giving any encryption option is given, or to add or change
the pass phrase by setting them.
These options can only be used with PEM format output files.
.It Sy -text
Prints out the public, private key components and parameters.
.It Sy -noout
This option prevents output of the encoded version of the key.
.It Sy -modulus
This option prints out the value of the public key component of the key.
.It Sy -pubin
By default, a private key is read from the input.
With this option a public key is read instead.
If the input contains no public key but a private key, its public part
is used.
.It Sy -pubout
By default, a private key is output.
With this option a public key will be output instead.
This option is automatically set if the input is a public key.
.It Sy -pvk-strong
Enable 'Strong' PVK encoding level (default).
.It Sy -pvk-weak
Enable 'Weak' PVK encoding level.
.It Sy -pvk-none
Don't enforce PVK encoding.
.It Sy -engine Em id
See
.Xr openssl 1 .
This option is deprecated.
.It Sy -provider Em name
.It Sy -provider-path Em path
.It Sy -provparam Em [name:]key=value
.It Sy -propquery Em propq
See
.Xr openssl 1 ,
.Xr provider 7 ,
and
.Xr property 7 .
.El
.Pp
The
.Xr openssl-pkey 1
command is capable of performing all the operations this command can, as
well as supporting other public key types.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The documentation for the
.Xr openssl-pkey 1
command contains examples equivalent to the ones listed here.
.Pp
To remove the pass phrase on a DSA private key:
.Bd -literal
 openssl dsa -in key.pem -out keyout.pem
.Ed
.Pp
To encrypt a private key using triple DES:
.Bd -literal
 openssl dsa -in key.pem -des3 -out keyout.pem
.Ed
.Pp
To convert a private key from PEM to DER format:
.Bd -literal
 openssl dsa -in key.pem -outform DER -out keyout.der
.Ed
.Pp
To print out the components of a private key to standard output:
.Bd -literal
 openssl dsa -in key.pem -text -noout
.Ed
.Pp
To just output the public part of a private key:
.Bd -literal
 openssl dsa -in key.pem -pubout -out pubkey.pem
.Ed
